{"task_id": "HumanEval/13", "solution": "\n\ndef greatest_common_divisor(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\" Return a greatest common divisor of two integers a and b\n    >>> greatest_common_divisor(3, 5)\n    1\n    >>> greatest_common_divisor(25, 15)\n    5\n    \"\"\"\nHere's the completion of the function using the Euclidean algorithm:\n\n```python\n    while b != 0:\n        a, b = b, a % b\n    return abs(a)\n```\n\nThis implements the Euclidean algorithm, which repeatedly replaces the larger number with the remainder of dividing the larger by the smaller until one becomes zero. The `abs()` ensures we return a positive value regardless of the signs of the inputs."}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/13", "solution": "\n\ndef greatest_common_divisor(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\" Return a greatest common divisor of two integers a and b\n    >>> greatest_common_divisor(3, 5)\n    1\n    >>> greatest_common_divisor(25, 15)\n    5\n    \"\"\"\nHere's the completion of the function using the Euclidean algorithm:\n\n```python\n    while b != 0:\n        a, b = b, a % b\n    return abs(a)\n```\n\nThis implementation:\n1. Uses the Euclidean algorithm which repeatedly replaces the larger number with the remainder of dividing the larger by the smaller\n2. Continues until b becomes 0\n3. Returns the absolute value of a to handle negative inputs correctly"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/10", "solution": "\n\ndef is_palindrome(string: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\" Test if given string is a palindrome \"\"\"\n    return string == string[::-1]\n\n\ndef make_palindrome(string: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\" Find the shortest palindrome that begins with a supplied string.\n    Algorithm idea is simple:\n    - Find the longest postfix of supplied string that is a palindrome.\n    - Append to the end of the string reverse of a string prefix that comes before the palindromic suffix.\n    >>> make_palindrome('')\n    ''\n    >>> make_palindrome('cat')\n    'catac'\n    >>> make_palindrome('cata')\n    'catac'\n    \"\"\"\nHere's the completion of the `make_palindrome` function:\n\n```python\ndef make_palindrome(string: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\" Find the shortest palindrome that begins with a supplied string.\n    Algorithm idea is simple:\n    - Find the longest postfix of supplied string that is a palindrome.\n    - Append to the end of the string reverse of a string prefix that comes before the palindromic suffix.\n    >>> make_palindrome('')\n    ''\n    >>> make_palindrome('cat')\n    'catac'\n    >>> make_palindrome('cata')\n    'catac'\n    \"\"\"\n    if not string:\n        return ''\n    \n    # Find the longest postfix that is a palindrome\n    for i in range(len(string)):\n        if is_palindrome(string[i:]):\n            # Found the longest palindromic suffix starting at index i\n            # Append the reverse of the prefix (before index i) to the end\n            return string + string[:i][::-1]\n    \n    # If no palindromic suffix found (shouldn't happen as single char is palindrome)\n    return string\n```\n\nThe algorithm works by:\n1. Starting from the beginning of the string, check each suffix to see if it's a palindrome\n2. When we find the longest palindromic suffix (first one we encounter), we take the prefix before it\n3. Reverse that prefix and append it to the original string\n4. This creates the shortest palindrome that starts with the original string\n\nFor example, with 'cat':\n- Check if 'cat' is palindrome: No\n- Check if 'at' is palindrome: No  \n- Check if 't' is palindrome: Yes\n- Prefix before 't' is 'ca'\n- Reverse of 'ca' is 'ac'\n- Result: 'cat' + 'ac' = 'catac'"}
