{"task_id": "HumanEval/33", "solution": "\n\ndef sort_third(l: list):\n    \"\"\"This function takes a list l and returns a list l' such that\n    l' is identical to l in the indicies that are not divisible by three, while its values at the indicies that are divisible by three are equal\n    to the values of the corresponding indicies of l, but sorted.\n    >>> sort_third([1, 2, 3])\n    [1, 2, 3]\n    >>> sort_third([5, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 2])\n    [2, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 5]\n    \"\"\"\nLooking at this problem, I need to:\n\n1. Extract elements at indices divisible by 3 (0, 3, 6, 9, ...)\n2. Sort those elements\n3. Put them back at their positions while keeping other elements unchanged\n\nLet me trace through the example:\n- `[5, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 2]`\n- Indices divisible by 3: 0, 3, 6 \u2192 values are 5, 4, 2\n- Sorted: [2, 4, 5]\n- Put back: index 0\u21922, index 3\u21924, index 6\u21925\n- Result: `[2, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 5]` \u2713\n\nHere's the solution:\n\n```python\ndef sort_third(l: list):\n    if len(l) == 0:\n        return []\n    \n    # Extract elements at indices divisible by 3\n    third_elements = [l[i] for i in range(0, len(l), 3)]\n    \n    # Sort them\n    third_elements.sort()\n    \n    # Create result list as a copy\n    result = l[:]\n    \n    # Place sorted elements back at indices divisible by 3\n    for idx, i in enumerate(range(0, len(l), 3)):\n        result[i] = third_elements[idx]\n    \n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/33", "solution": "\n\ndef sort_third(l: list):\n    \"\"\"This function takes a list l and returns a list l' such that\n    l' is identical to l in the indicies that are not divisible by three, while its values at the indicies that are divisible by three are equal\n    to the values of the corresponding indicies of l, but sorted.\n    >>> sort_third([1, 2, 3])\n    [1, 2, 3]\n    >>> sort_third([5, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 2])\n    [2, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 5]\n    \"\"\"\nLooking at this problem, I need to:\n1. Extract elements at indices divisible by 3 (0, 3, 6, 9, ...)\n2. Sort those elements\n3. Put the sorted elements back at those same indices\n4. Keep all other elements unchanged\n\nLet me trace through the second example:\n- Input: `[5, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 2]`\n- Indices divisible by 3: 0, 3, 6\n- Elements at those indices: 5 (index 0), 4 (index 3), 2 (index 6)\n- Sorted: [2, 4, 5]\n- Put back: 2 at index 0, 4 at index 3, 5 at index 6\n- Result: `[2, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 5]`\n\nHere's the solution:\n\n```python\ndef sort_third(l: list):\n    if len(l) == 0:\n        return []\n    \n    # Extract elements at indices divisible by 3\n    third_elements = [l[i] for i in range(0, len(l), 3)]\n    \n    # Sort them\n    third_elements.sort()\n    \n    # Create result list as a copy of original\n    result = l.copy()\n    \n    # Put sorted elements back at indices divisible by 3\n    for i, val in enumerate(third_elements):\n        result[i * 3] = val\n    \n    return result\n```"}
