{"task_id": "HumanEval/38", "solution": "\n\ndef encode_cyclic(s: str):\n    \"\"\"\n    returns encoded string by cycling groups of three characters.\n    \"\"\"\n    # split string to groups. Each of length 3.\n    groups = [s[(3 * i):min((3 * i + 3), len(s))] for i in range((len(s) + 2) // 3)]\n    # cycle elements in each group. Unless group has fewer elements than 3.\n    groups = [(group[1:] + group[0]) if len(group) == 3 else group for group in groups]\n    return \"\".join(groups)\n\n\ndef decode_cyclic(s: str):\n    \"\"\"\n    takes as input string encoded with encode_cyclic function. Returns decoded string.\n    \"\"\"\nLooking at the `encode_cyclic` function, I need to understand what it does:\n1. It splits the string into groups of 3 characters\n2. For groups with exactly 3 characters, it cycles them by moving the first character to the end (e.g., \"abc\" \u2192 \"bca\")\n3. Groups with fewer than 3 characters remain unchanged\n\nTo decode, I need to reverse this operation. For a group of 3 characters that was cycled, I need to move the last character to the beginning (e.g., \"bca\" \u2192 \"abc\").\n\nIf encoding does: `group[1:] + group[0]` (move first to end)\nThen decoding should do: `group[-1] + group[:-1]` (move last to beginning)\n\nAlternatively, I can apply the cycle operation twice more (since cycling 3 times returns to original).\n\nHere's the solution:\n\n```python\ndef decode_cyclic(s: str):\n    \"\"\"\n    takes as input string encoded with encode_cyclic function. Returns decoded string.\n    \"\"\"\n    # split string to groups. Each of length 3.\n    groups = [s[(3 * i):min((3 * i + 3), len(s))] for i in range((len(s) + 2) // 3)]\n    # reverse cycle elements in each group. Unless group has fewer elements than 3.\n    groups = [(group[-1] + group[:-1]) if len(group) == 3 else group for group in groups]\n    return \"\".join(groups)\n```"}
