{"task_id": "HumanEval/41", "solution": "\n\ndef car_race_collision(n: int):\n    \"\"\"\n    Imagine a road that's a perfectly straight infinitely long line.\n    n cars are driving left to right;  simultaneously, a different set of n cars\n    are driving right to left.   The two sets of cars start out being very far from\n    each other.  All cars move in the same speed.  Two cars are said to collide\n    when a car that's moving left to right hits a car that's moving right to left.\n    However, the cars are infinitely sturdy and strong; as a result, they continue moving\n    in their trajectory as if they did not collide.\n\n    This function outputs the number of such collisions.\n    \"\"\"\nLooking at this problem, I need to think about what happens when two sets of n cars are moving toward each other.\n\nKey observations:\n1. n cars are moving left to right\n2. n cars are moving right to left\n3. All cars move at the same speed\n4. Cars pass through each other (don't stop on collision)\n5. We need to count total collisions\n\nSince the cars are moving toward each other and pass through each other:\n- Each car moving left to right will eventually meet and collide with EACH car moving right to left\n- This is because they're on the same straight line and moving toward each other\n- Since cars pass through each other, every possible pair will collide exactly once\n\nTherefore:\n- Each of the n left-to-right cars will collide with each of the n right-to-left cars\n- Total collisions = n \u00d7 n = n\u00b2\n\n```python\ndef car_race_collision(n: int):\n    \"\"\"\n    Imagine a road that's a perfectly straight infinitely long line.\n    n cars are driving left to right;  simultaneously, a different set of n cars\n    are driving right to left.   The two sets of cars start out being very far from\n    each other.  All cars move in the same speed.  Two cars are said to collide\n    when a car that's moving left to right hits a car that's moving right to left.\n    However, the cars are infinitely sturdy and strong; as a result, they continue moving\n    in their trajectory as if they did not collide.\n\n    This function outputs the number of such collisions.\n    \"\"\"\n    return n * n\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/43", "solution": "\n\ndef pairs_sum_to_zero(l):\n    \"\"\"\n    pairs_sum_to_zero takes a list of integers as an input.\n    it returns True if there are two distinct elements in the list that\n    sum to zero, and False otherwise.\n    >>> pairs_sum_to_zero([1, 3, 5, 0])\n    False\n    >>> pairs_sum_to_zero([1, 3, -2, 1])\n    False\n    >>> pairs_sum_to_zero([1, 2, 3, 7])\n    False\n    >>> pairs_sum_to_zero([2, 4, -5, 3, 5, 7])\n    True\n    >>> pairs_sum_to_zero([1])\n    False\n    \"\"\"\nLooking at this problem, I need to find if there are two distinct elements in the list that sum to zero.\n\nThe key insight is that two numbers sum to zero if one is the negative of the other. So I can:\n1. Iterate through the list\n2. For each element, check if its negative exists in the list\n3. Make sure we're not comparing an element with itself\n\nHere's an efficient approach using a set:\n\n```python\ndef pairs_sum_to_zero(l):\n    seen = set()\n    for num in l:\n        if -num in seen:\n            return True\n        seen.add(num)\n    return False\n```\n\nThis works by:\n- Maintaining a set of numbers we've seen so far\n- For each number, checking if its negative is already in the set\n- If we find such a pair, return True immediately\n- If we finish the loop without finding a pair, return False\n\nThis handles the \"distinct elements\" requirement because we only add a number to `seen` after checking, so we never compare a number with itself."}
